WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.463
(ambient music)

00:00:01.463 --> 00:00:04.274
- [Voiceover] This is Amali.

00:00:04.274 --> 00:00:05.524
She's an addax.

00:00:07.619 --> 00:00:09.369
She's also a new mom.

00:00:11.982 --> 00:00:15.288
Her calf Adeline is just three weeks old

00:00:15.288 --> 00:00:18.476
and she's very important because the addax

00:00:18.476 --> 00:00:21.393
is a critically endangered species.

00:00:24.714 --> 00:00:28.517
The addax is a native of the
Sahara Desert, and once roamed

00:00:28.517 --> 00:00:31.184
over vast areas of north Africa.

00:00:32.081 --> 00:00:35.062
They're also known as
the screw horn antelope.

00:00:35.062 --> 00:00:36.812
It's easy to see why.

00:00:38.502 --> 00:00:41.266
They're extremely well
adapted to life in the desert.

00:00:41.266 --> 00:00:44.476
Their white coats reflect
the scorching sun,

00:00:44.476 --> 00:00:47.160
and their splayed hooves
are perfectly tailored

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:49.844
for traversing desert sands.

00:00:49.844 --> 00:00:52.522
The addax can also survive
extremely long periods

00:00:52.522 --> 00:00:54.430
without water.

00:00:54.430 --> 00:00:58.110
But these adaptations are
no help against humans.

00:00:58.110 --> 00:01:02.288
The addax has been hunted
nearly into oblivion.

00:01:02.288 --> 00:01:06.659
Estimates put their population
at less than 200 individuals

00:01:06.659 --> 00:01:09.651
in just a few pockets of the Sahara.

00:01:09.651 --> 00:01:12.676
Luckily, reintroduction
efforts and captive breeding

00:01:12.676 --> 00:01:16.284
programs like the one that
produced little Adaline

00:01:16.284 --> 00:01:18.225
are helping the survival of this species

00:01:18.225 --> 00:01:21.591
that has all but disappeared
from the face of the Earth.

00:01:21.591 --> 00:01:23.091
This is the addax.

